romanized_response:
HTTP status code 429 Too Many Requests ka matlab hai ki server par ek rate limit hai aur aapki request usse zyada ho gayi hai. Iske peeche yeh kuch reasons ho sakte hain:

1. Jis code se yeh request ki ja rahi thi, usse kam samay mein bahut baar run kiya gaya, jiski wajah se rate limit exceed ho gayi.

2. Website un requests ko reject kar deti hai jinhe woh spam samajhti hai, 429 status code ke saath. Iska matlab yeh ho sakta hai ki user agent ek common browser nahi hai, jo kuch websites ek simple check ke taur par use karti hain. Isko theek karne ke liye, aap GET request mein "User-Agent" header add kar sakte hain, jaise ki:

```
const response = await axios.get('https://www.realestate.com.au/rent/in-sydney,+nsw/list-1', 
     { headers: { 'User-Agent': 'browser.version' }  }
);
```

Note karein ki aapko `"browser.version"` ko ek actual browser user agent string se replace karna hoga. Jis application mein yeh code run ho raha hai, uske hisaab se, isse user agent spoofing mana ja sakta hai, jo kuch countries ya organizations ke laws (ya us website ki terms of service jise code access karne ki koshish kar raha hai) ke तहत restricted ho sakta hai.

3. Website kisi aur reason se requests ko rate limit kar rahi hai. Yeh is wajah se ho sakta hai ki request ek proxy ke through ki ja rahi hai, jismein proxy ke doosre users ne bahut zyada requests ki hongi, हालांकि yeh unlikely hai.

4. Website sabhi requests ko reject kar rahi hai kyunki us par denial of service attack ho raha hai. Yeh unlikely hai, kyunki aise attack ke under ek server ka typical behavior traffic ko ek non-existent server par route karna ya use poori tarah se deny karna hoga, jisse koi valid response nahi milega. Lekin, agar website HTTP status code ke saath respond bhi karti hai, toh woh shayad server error 503 Service Unavailable hoga, jo tab diya jata hai jab server overloaded hota hai.

response_en:
Yes, certainly! The issue you're encountering is because the string literals "fizzbuzz", "fizz", and "buzz" are temporary values, and you are trying to return a reference to them, which is not allowed because they will be dropped at the end of the scope, and the returned reference would be dangling.

You can fix this error by returning &'static str instead of &str. &'static str refers to string literals, which have a 'static lifetime, meaning they last for the duration of the program. Here's an updated version of your code:

fn fizzbuzz(n: usize) -> &'static str {
    match (n % 3, n % 5) {
        (0, 0) => "fizzbuzz",
        (0, _) => "fizz",
        (_, 0) => "buzz",
        _ => {
            let s = n.to_string();
            Box::leak(s.into_boxed_str())
        }
    }
}

Note that the last arm returns a &'static str that is created from a String using the Box::leak function, which takes ownership of a Box<T> and returns a pointer to its contents, effectively leaking it. This is safe because the returned pointer will last for the duration of the program, but it should be used with care as it will not be cleaned up automatically.

romanized_response:
Haan, bilkul! Jo issue aapko aa raha hai, woh isliye hai kyunki string literals "fizzbuzz", "fizz", aur "buzz" temporary values hain, aur aap unka reference return karne ki koshish kar rahe hain, jo allowed nahi hai kyunki woh scope ke end mein drop ho jayenge, aur jo reference return hoga woh dangling ho jayega.

Aap is error ko &'static str return karke theek kar sakte hain, na ki &str. &'static str string literals ko refer karta hai, jinki 'static lifetime hoti hai, matlab woh program ki duration tak rehte hain. Yahan aapke code ka updated version hai:

fn fizzbuzz(n: usize) -> &'static str {
    match (n % 3, n % 5) {
        (0, 0) => "fizzbuzz",
        (0, _) => "fizz",
        (_, 0) => "buzz",
        _ => {
            let s = n.to_string();
            Box::leak(s.into_boxed_str())
        }
    }
}

Note karein ki last arm ek &'static str return karta hai jo Box::leak function ka use karke ek String se banaya gaya hai, jo Box<T> ki ownership leta hai aur uske contents ka pointer return karta hai, effectively use leak kar deta hai. Yeh safe hai kyunki return kiya gaya pointer program ki duration tak rahega, lekin iska use dhyan se karna chahiye kyunki yeh automatically clean up nahi hoga.

response_en:
Merge sort is a divide and conquer algorithm that works by recursively splitting the input array in half until the arrays are split into single elements. Then, the sorted arrays are merged back together in sorted order.

The key benefits of merge sort over a simpler algorithm like bubble sort are:

1. It is a stable sort that does not change the relative order of elements with equal keys.
2. It has an efficiency of O(n log n) time complexity, making it a relatively efficient algorithm even for large input sizes. Bubble sort, on the other hand, has a quadratic time complexity of O(n^2), making it very inefficient for larger inputs.
3. It is a generic algorithm that can sort any type of data for which a comparison function exists.

Here is a Rust implementation example:
```rust
fn merge_sort(vec: &mut [i32]) {
    if vec.len() < 2 {
        return;
    }

    let mid = vec.len() / 2;
    let (left, right) = vec.split_at_mut(mid);

    merge_sort(left);
    merge_sort(right);

    merge(left, right, vec);
}

fn merge(left: &mut [i32], right: &mut [i32], vec: &mut [i32]) {
    let mut i = 0;
    let mut j = 0;
    let mut k = 0;

    while i < left.len() && j < right.len() {
        if left[i] < right[j] {
            vec[k] = left[i];
            i += 1;
        } else {
            vec[k] = right[j];
            j += 1;
        }
        k += 1;
    }

    while i < left.len() {
        vec[k] = left[i];
        i += 1;
        k += 1;
    }

    while j < right.len() {
        vec[k] = right[j];
        j += 1;
        k += 1;
    }
}
```

romanized_response:
Merge sort ek divide and conquer algorithm hai jo input array ko recursively aadha-aadha split karke kaam karta hai, jab tak ki arrays single elements mein split na ho jayein. Phir, sorted arrays ko wapas sorted order mein merge kiya jata hai.

Bubble sort jaise simpler algorithm ke mukable merge sort ke main fayde yeh hain:

1.  Yeh ek stable sort hai jo equal keys wale elements ke relative order ko nahi badalta hai.
2.  Ismein O(n log n) time complexity ki efficiency hai, jo ise bade input sizes ke liye bhi ek relatively efficient algorithm banati hai. Dusri taraf, bubble sort ki quadratic time complexity O(n^2) hoti hai, jo ise bade inputs ke liye bahut inefficient banati hai.
3.  Yeh ek generic algorithm hai jo kisi bhi type ke data ko sort kar sakta hai jiske liye comparison function exist karta ho.

Yahan ek Rust implementation example hai:
```rust
fn merge_sort(vec: &mut [i32]) {
    if vec.len() < 2 {
        return;
    }

    let mid = vec.len() / 2;
    let (left, right) = vec.split_at_mut(mid);

    merge_sort(left);
    merge_sort(right);

    merge(left, right, vec);
}

fn merge(left: &mut [i32], right: &mut [i32], vec: &mut [i32]) {
    let mut i = 0;
    let mut j = 0;
    let mut k = 0;

    while i < left.len() && j < right.len() {
        if left[i] < right[j] {
            vec[k] = left[i];
            i += 1;
        } else {
            vec[k] = right[j];
            j += 1;
        }
        k += 1;
    }

    while i < left.len() {
        vec[k] = left[i];
        i += 1;
        k += 1;
    }

    while j < right.len() {
        vec[k] = right[j];
        j += 1;
        k += 1;
    }
}
```
